1 MCP Terminal Guide
********************

# MCP Terminal - Full API And Operations Guide

## Purpose

MCP Terminal exposes a project-aware terminal API through MCP Proxy. It gives
agents a repeatable way to run commands against a watched project, read output,
preserve per-session shell state, and clean up resources.

Normal work runs in Docker sandbox containers with the project mounted at
`/workspace`. Interactive PTY commands attach to an already kept sandbox
container. A separate, explicitly enabled `terminal_host_exec` path can run
configured commands on the real host via SSH.

This document is the canonical guide returned by the MCP `info` command and
installed by the Debian package as
`/usr/share/doc/mcp-terminal-docker/MCP_TERMINAL_INFO.md`. For exact JSON
schemas and command-specific examples, call `help(command="<command_name>")`.

## API Contract

The server is an HTTPS MCP adapter service. All public operations are MCP
commands registered by `mcp_terminal.term_server`; the first command is `info`.
The `info` command has no parameters and returns:

| Field | Meaning |
|-------|---------|
| `guide_version` | Version of this API guide |
| `package` | Python project, Debian package, and image tag metadata |
| `summary` | One-line lifecycle reminder |
| `markdown` | This full guide |
| `lifecycle` | Ordered high-level workflow steps |
| `registered_commands` | Live command catalog generated from registered command classes |
| `docs` | Host-side man/info/documentation paths |

The command catalog is live. It is not a hand-maintained list in `info_command`.
If a command is added to `_TERMINAL_COMMAND_TYPES`, it appears in `info`.

## Installed Package

The Debian package is `mcp-terminal-docker`.

Host-side package commands:

| Command | Role |
|---------|------|
| `mcp-terminal-docker status` | Show systemd/container status and image metadata |
| `mcp-terminal-docker recreate` | Recreate the service container after config changes |
| `mcp-terminal-docker info` | Same operator summary as `mcp-terminal-info` |
| `mcp-terminal-info` | Host integration summary |
| `mcp-terminal-preflight` | Validate config, TLS, Docker, adapter imports, and host integration |

Host-side documentation:

| Document | Role |
|----------|------|
| `man mcp-terminal-docker` | Service wrapper commands |
| `man mcp-terminal-info` | Host integration summary command |
| `man mcp-terminal-preflight` | Preflight command |
| `man mcp-terminal-config` | Config file reference |
| `info mcp-terminal-docker` | GNU info manual generated from this guide at package build time |
| `/usr/share/doc/mcp-terminal-docker/MCP_TERMINAL_INFO.md` | This MCP/API guide |

## Architecture

The service container exposes an HTTPS MCP adapter and registers itself in MCP
Proxy. It reads `/etc/mcp-terminal/term_server.json`, discovers projects from
configured watch directories, and manages `.terminals/<session_id>/` state under
each project.

The service has three execution targets:

| Target | Command Family | Boundary |
|--------|----------------|----------|
| Sandbox | `terminal_run` | Docker container, project mounted at `/workspace` |
| Sandbox PTY | `terminal_attach`, `terminal_send`, `terminal_read_shell` | Interactive process inside a kept sandbox container |
| Real host | `terminal_host_exec` | SSH from the service container to configured host user |

Sandbox and host execution are intentionally separate. `terminal_run` never
executes directly on the real host. `terminal_host_exec` never uses the sandbox
container lifecycle.

## Identifiers

MCP Terminal uses three important identifiers:

| Identifier | Source | Used By |
|------------|--------|---------|
| `project_id` | Project registry / watch catalog | Project-scoped terminal commands |
| `session_id` | Code Analysis Server `session_create` result | Project terminal session state |
| `host_run_id` | Generated by `terminal_host_exec` in sessionless mode | Host execution status/output commands |

For project-scoped sandbox work, use `(project_id, session_id)` everywhere.
For sessionless host execution, omit both `project_id` and `session_id` when
calling `terminal_host_exec`; the response includes `host_run_id`, which is then
used for `terminal_get_status`, `terminal_read`, and `terminal_tail`.

